; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sockets, over ws2_32.
;
; A SOCKET is handle-sized and never 0, 1 or 2, so `winHandleOf` passes
; it through, and `platformClose` asks `getsockopt(SO_TYPE)` whether a
; handle is one before choosing `closesocket` over `CloseHandle`.
; Sockets are made with `WSASocketW` and WSA_FLAG_OVERLAPPED, because
; Winsock keeps a receive or send timeout (SO_RCVTIMEO, SO_SNDTIMEO)
; only on an overlapped socket: a blocking `recv` on one made without
; the flag waits in the kernel with no timeout to apply, and
; `tests/stdlib/843-net-stream.ax` sat in its first timed read until
; the runner stopped it. `recv` and `send`, which `platformReadFd` and
; `platformWriteFd` route a socket to, block as before. `WSAStartup` is counted
; per call by Winsock and the process's exit undoes the count, so every
; `platformSocket` asks for 2.2 rather than keeping a global the
; language has no place for.
;
; Readiness is `WSAPoll`: IO.ax's poller keeps its own table of
; registrations on this target and hands them here as POLLRDNORM and
; POLLWRNORM interests, which come back as readiness in the same
; records.
